1 OCCU Surgeon
1 TITL Dr.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Legendary Locals: Dr. Halvor Holte and the 'Little Mayo of the North'
2 CONT Kristina Gray
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Halvor Holte
2 CONT     Dr. Halvor Holte will end this ten week series about Crookston’s notables. He is another Norwegian, like Halvor Steenerson, who I wrote about several weeks ago. I’ve been informed by Nancy Melby, who did research about the Hol
2 CONC tes including interviews with them, that the family name of “Holte” was pronounced in two syllables with the “e” at the end. Dr. Halvor Holte was a long time doctor in Crookston. Before that he broke sod and felled trees in Marsha
2 CONC ll County. Many Norwegians, like him, came to Minnesota at the beck and call of the Great Northern railroad baron, James J. Hill. Hill needed people like the Steenersons and Holtes to populate the great, untapped area of northern 
2 CONC Minnesota, in order to make his railroad to thunder through the empty valley, viable and profitable.  This did not come without human cost and sacrifice of life, especially in the Holte household.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Halvor was born on July 11, 1857 in Selland, Stavanger. He died on Jan. 2, 1937, almost an octogenarian, after two days of illness of pneumonia. Funeral services were held at the English Lutheran church. In 1929, when reflecti
2 CONC ng about his own full life, Halvor Holte wrote that in the spring of 1873, when he was 16 years old, he left Norway with his parents and three brothers (Alf, Ole Johan and Martin) for the U.S. After crossing the Atlantic, the fami
2 CONC ly ended up in Quebec, Canada, then Illinois. Finally, once at a standstill, the Holte parents were able to claim 160 acres of land in Fillmore County in Minnesota, close to Beaver, Minnesota.
2 CONT
2 CONT     In 1882, when Halvor was only 25 years old, he filed a tree claim ten miles from Milbank, South Dakota. Many Norwegians had adventured in a westerly direction, think O.E. Rolvaag’s “Giants in the Earth.” However, I found docum
2 CONC entation that Halvor later traversed north with another group of Norwegian settlers, 12 miles north of Thief River Falls and filed on land.  In fact, the township was organized and named after Halvor Holte, but simply spelled “Hol
2 CONC t” changing its original name from “Sandridge.”
2 CONT
2 CONT     That gives you an idea about how the lay of the land was, sandy and perhaps on an old beach of Lake Agassiz. At least, not good for farming like we are used to around the Crookston area. I read elsewhere that the town of Holt,
2 CONC  Minnesota used to have a population of 232 people according to the 1920 census. It still has a zip code of 56738 but is well below 75 people.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The remarkable thing about this Norwegian was Holte built a log cabin of 14 x 16 feet where he had broken some acres of land. Halvor got the deed for it in spring of 1883 when he went to Crookston.  While in his northern cabin
2 CONC , he spent his spare time in reading, not talking to anyone for weeks at a time. The LaBree, Minnesota post office was 14 miles away.  His life of surviving in the woods was fairly simple. He only had an almanac, hymnbook and New 
2 CONC Testament to read. In fact, he memorized what little reading material he had.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Halvor Holte had already been acquainted with the Crookston area when he had come earlier in May of 1881. He wanted to go to the university but had little money for tuition. Therefore, he drove an ox cart from Fillmore County to t
2 CONC he Red River wheat fields in order to make money for his schooling.  On Thanksgiving Day of 1883, by the age of 26, he set out to go to Augsburg seminary.  All of his educational training came with hard work, doing odd jobs to pay
2 CONC  his tuition; nothing was handed to him on a silver platter. In the fall of 1886, Halvor went to St. Olaf and graduated in 1888 at age 31. Then in 1890, Halvor went to the University of Minnesota Medical School and graduated as a 
2 CONC medical doctor in June 1893.  The next month, July 15 1893, he re-located in Crookston to set up his practice as a 36 year old, pioneer doctor who had already experienced the wilds of northwest Minnesota.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Even though Dr. Holte lived in Crookston with his doctor’s office on 105 North Broadway, that didn’t mean that he did not have to make house calls to those who were off in the far reaches of Polk County. I read Win V. Working’
2 CONC s thoughts on “country doctors” in the Crookston Daily Times Sept. 12, 1935. It explains why Dr. Halvor Holte may have been so highly esteemed and also highly motivated to create a hospital in Crookston. “Early ‘country doctors’ c
2 CONC alled on patients under any weather conditions, the country had not [yet] been settled with good roads.  Another feature of the pioneer doctor’s service was that he also was called on to exercise his humane tendencies. Often he wa
2 CONC s the family confidant and adviser.  And because of this intimate relationship he was endeared to the families he served. Perhaps it is for this reason chiefly that the memory of the country doctor is revered.”
2 CONT
2 CONT     When Dr. Halvor Holte was interviewed by the Crookston Daily Times, his advice to young people was, “Get an education, get something worthwhile to do, get married.”  Halvor was first married to Ida Josephine Loe in early 1890s
2 CONC , who was originally from Minneapolis. She died of consumption on November 7, 1899. Their children were Harold Oliver who was born on March 12, 1896 and Evelyn Theresa was almost two years old when she died earlier of consumption 
2 CONC on July 30, 1899. Unfortunately, Harold Oliver Holte was born with a crippling disability which they believe was due to polio. Harold graduated from Central High School in 1917 and he died in 1926. He was 30 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The second marriage for Dr. Holte was to Henrietta Lunde. Their children were Evelyn Irene and Junius Augustine. Henrietta Lunde Holte was an amazing woman in her own right. She was not just a doctor’s wife, actually Henrietta
2 CONC  Holte needs a whole column dedicated to just her life. I hope to write about the second Mrs. Holte later because she was a well-travelled lady and had a huge heart for the native American Indians population in the area.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The next question Halvor was asked by the Crookston Daily Times was: “What is Crookston’s greatest need?”  Halvor’s answer was, “Harmony and cooperation of her citizens.” I think he may have answered in this way because he had
2 CONC  witnessed the unnecessary, adverse politics going on in Crookston and Polk County. Only once did he step into the fray to run as Crookston’s mayor. He lost but perhaps his heart was not in it.  He was more civic minded in startin
2 CONC g the Bethesda Hospital and also eventually his home from 1900-1920 expanded to become the Northwestern Clinic on South Broadway. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     In 1896, Dr. Holte purchased a large residence for the new Bethesda hospital; it was the late Sheriff Paulsrud’s house on Woodland Avenue. Bethesda is the Biblical term which means “Pool of Healing.” Actually, I read, “Bethesd
2 CONC a Hospital was organized March 31, 1898 by five ministers and seven laymen belonging to the then United Norwegian Lutheran church of America.  Any person of good reputation could become a member of this Association by paying $1.00
2 CONC  a year or $10 once to become a life time member.”
2 CONT
2 CONT     By 1898 Dr. Holte built an operating room and four wards which made room for 20 patients. On March 10, 1899, the Bethesda Hospital started with Drs. Loe (his brother-in-law) and Holte as the physicians in charge. (The Benedict
2 CONC ine sisters had the same idea of building the St. Vincent Hospital for the Catholic population on Seventh Street in 1902.) I read elsewhere words written about the mission statement for the Bethesda Hospital on Nov. 24, 1902, “The
2 CONC  motive of founders and attending sisters is self-sacrificing love, and the institution is doing the work of the good Samaritan. The aim of the institution is to show mercy to the sick and suffering, to take care of them and to af
2 CONC ford them spiritual consolation, and help the worthy poor in their sickness. This institution therefore commends itself especially to all those who are imbued with love to God and fellow men.”
2 CONT
2 CONT     Perhaps a direct result of losing his first wife, Josephine, and their daughter to tuberculosis the year earlier in 1899, Dr. Holte assisted in 1900 to organize the Polk County Anti-Tuberculosis Society. Eventually, by 1914, h
2 CONC e helped start the Sunnyrest Tuberculosis sanitarium on College Avenue, close to the present RiverView Hospital that used to be Bethesda Hospital.  
2 CONT
2 CONT     Henrietta Lunde, who was born in November 26, 1871 in Fairfax, Minnesota, attended the University of Minnesota and taught school in Fairfax, Hector and St. Hilaire, Minnesota. The Holtes were married in 1902 when Dr. Holte was
2 CONC  45 years and Henrietta was 31. Henrietta’s brother, Rev. Alexander Lunde, as officiating pastor, married them at the Maple Lake Lutheran church. He was Dr. Holte’s friend but then he also became his brother-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT     According to Halvor and Henrietta’s daughter, Evelyn Holte, “Father was a very religious man. The family attended services every Sunday. He was active in the [Lutheran] church, but kept out of politics there. Often the usher w
2 CONC ould come to tell Father that he had an emergency call. He would tell Mother where and quietly leave the pew… Father was always there to give a helping hand to anyone who needed his help. He did not neglect his family, but as most
2 CONC  Norwegians, he seldom showed emotions, but we knew how much he loved each of us.”
2 CONT
2 CONT     “One fine memory I have of Father was that he would sit at the piano with me on his lap and sing Norwegian songs: “Per Spillerman or Peter the Musician.” Later, as he sat on a chair with knees crossed, I would sit on his foot 
2 CONC while he sang “Ride ride Ranken Ride Ride on Ranken.”  Father had learned to swim on “Holtah vandet” Holtah water, where he swam and fished. When he came to our cottage at Maple Lake, he would get into his swim suit and dive into 
2 CONC the lake from our dock and swim far into the lake. If there was any daylight we too would swim around the dock, we never ventured far out.”
2 CONT
2 CONT     In a Crookston Daily Times article dated May 24, 1953, when Dr. Halvor Holte’s widow was asked about the early days of the Bethesda Hospital, Henrietta claimed, “one of the hardest jobs of all was to get sick people to put the
2 CONC ir trust in a hospital. In those days people thought that once in a hospital all hope was given up for saving their life.  The idea of going to a hospital with the real intention of getting well had to be promoted and worked on by
2 CONC  Dr. Holte and the others who used the hospital.”
2 CONT
2 CONT     In March 19, 1908, the hospital records showed that 208 people had been cared for by the small staff of nurses and doctors.  It was important to show that: “154 were healed 48 improved 2 not improved, 8 died, 5 of those dying 
2 CONC admitted incurable, 95 operations, 8 childbirths, Norwegians – 162, Swedes – 23, Germans – 10, Native Americans - 6, Irish - 5, English - 1, Italian - 1.” By 1928, the Bethesda hospital expanded and contained 50 beds. When Sister 
2 CONC Anna Forlie arrived in Crookston to help Dr. Holte in 1911, she made sure a wing was added in 1920. Sister Forlie was an indispensable help and “served in almost any capacity, from that of janitor, cook, decorator, nurse, sister m
2 CONC other and superintendent for 15 years.”
2 CONT
2 CONT     Because Dr. Holte believed in education, he left for Germany to study in 1906, at the age 49. He believed he was never too old to learn. His parents Halvor and Turi and his brother Augustin A. came to stay with his wife Henrie
2 CONC tta and their three children Harold age 10, Junius age 3 ½ and Evelyn age 2, living at 220 South Broadway. By the time the new wing of the Bethesda Hospital was built in 1920, he sold his residence at 220 South Broadway to Northwe
2 CONC st Clinic and it was remodeled to house the clinic. He went out to California for more training and when he returned to Crookston, he purchased Halvor Steenerson’s former residence at 303 Houston Ave.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The following is what Dr. Holte’s daughter, Evelyn, wrote about her memory of the new clinic:  “When father married my mother [Henrietta], he built a large home at 220 South Broadway. Later, he put an addition on the front for
2 CONC  office space. My brother, Harold, built for him the first x-ray in the area.  Later the home was sold to Dr. Oscar Locken and was extensively remodeled and enlarged and it is now the Northwestern Clinic.  My father was active in 
2 CONC civic affairs and helped sponsor the construction of the Crookston Hotel, the Opera House, the Crookston Public Library (pre-Carnegie) and support other public ventures.”
2 CONT
2 CONT     Indeed, Crookston’s Northwest Clinic was organized on Oct. 10, 1920 by the founders, Dr. M.O. Oppegaard, Dr. O. Locken and Dr. C.D. Mitchell.   
2 CONT
2 CONT          Another person I want to honor is Miss Mae Rideout, who was one of the earlier administrators of the Northwest Clinic.  For now, Dr. Halvor Holte, whose white frame hospital became the original Bethesda hospital in 1898 a
2 CONC nd then Northwest Clinic had once been Dr. Holte’s residence. I would say that Dr. Halvor Holte’s contributions to Crookston were far reaching. In fact, little known but often said when Crookston’s population was bustling with act
2 CONC ivity, “There was a time when the old Bethesda [Hospital] was known as the “Little Mayo of the North.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Feb 6, 2025
2 CONT Kent, my mother Tillie Holte, niece of Johan and Dr. Halvor Holte
