2 CONC wegians, he seldom showed emotions, but we knew how much he loved each of us.”
2 CONT “One fine memory I have of Father was that he would sit at the piano with me on his lap and sing Norwegian songs: “Per Spillerman or Peter the Musician.” Later, as he sat on a chair with knees crossed, I would sit on his foot whil
2 CONC e he sang “Ride ride Ranken Ride Ride on Ranken.” Father had learned to swim on “Holtah vandet” Holtah water, where he swam and fished. When he came to our cottage at Maple Lake, he would get into his swim suit and dive into the l
2 CONC ake from our dock and swim far into the lake. If there was any daylight we too would swim around the dock, we never ventured far out.”
2 CONT In a Crookston Daily Times article dated May 24, 1953, when Dr. Halvor Holte’s widow was asked about the early days of the Bethesda Hospital, Henrietta claimed, “one of the hardest jobs of all was to get sick people to put their t
2 CONC rust in a hospital. In those days people thought that once in a hospital all hope was given up for saving their life. The idea of going to a hospital with the real intention of getting well had to be promoted and worked on by Dr. 
2 CONC Holte and the others who used the hospital.”
2 CONT In March 19, 1908, the hospital records showed that 208 people had been cared for by the small staff of nurses and doctors. It was important to show that: “154 were healed 48 improved 2 not improved, 8 died, 5 of those dying admit
2 CONC ted incurable, 95 operations, 8 childbirths, Norwegians – 162, Swedes – 23, Germans – 10, Native Americans - 6, Irish - 5, English - 1, Italian - 1.” By 1928, the Bethesda hospital expanded and contained 50 beds. When Sister Anna 
2 CONC Forlie arrived in Crookston to help Dr. Holte in 1911, she made sure a wing was added in 1920. Sister Forlie was an indispensable help and “served in almost any capacity, from that of janitor, cook, decorator, nurse, sister mother
2 CONC  and superintendent for 15 years.”
2 CONT Because Dr. Holte believed in education, he left for Germany to study in 1906, at the age 49. He believed he was never too old to learn. His parents Halvor and Turi and his brother Augustin A. came to stay with his wife Henrietta 
2 CONC and their three children Harold age 10, Junius age 3 ½ and Evelyn age 2, living at 220 South Broadway. By the time the new wing of the Bethesda Hospital was built in 1920, he sold his residence at 220 South Broadway to Northwest C
2 CONC linic and it was remodeled to house the clinic. He went out to California for more training and when he returned to Crookston, he purchased Halvor Steenerson’s former residence at 303 Houston Ave.
2 CONT The following is what Dr. Holte’s daughter, Evelyn, wrote about her memory of the new clinic: “When father married my mother [Henrietta], he built a large home at 220 South Broadway. Later, he put an addition on the front for offi
2 CONC ce space. My brother, Harold, built for him the first x-ray in the area. Later the home was sold to Dr. Oscar Locken and was extensively remodeled and enlarged and it is now the Northwestern Clinic. My father was active in civic a
2 CONC ffairs and helped sponsor the construction of the Crookston Hotel, the Opera House, the Crookston Public Library (pre-Carnegie) and support other public ventures.”
2 CONT Indeed, Crookston’s Northwest Clinic was organized on Oct. 10, 1920 by the founders, Dr. M.O. Oppegaard, Dr. O. Locken and Dr. C.D. Mitchell.
2 CONT Another person I want to honor is Miss Mae Rideout, who was one of the earlier administrators of the Northwest Clinic. For now, Dr. Halvor Holte, whose white frame hospital became the original Bethesda hospital in 1898 and then No
2 CONC rthwest Clinic had once been Dr. Holte’s residence. I would say that Dr. Halvor Holte’s contributions to Crookston were far reaching. In fact, little known but often said when Crookston’s population was bustling with activity, “Th
2 CONC ere was a time when the old Bethesda [Hospital] was known as the “Little Mayo of the North.
