2 NOTE I'm sorry I thought I wrote I agree with you before comment # 2 below. I wonder what happened to that.. They are probably the same house.  Love you.
2 NOTE What about a dormer and what about a second chimney - neither of these two pictures show anything like that,  These two pictures are almost certainly the same house. See the position of all of the windows. The window and the door 
3 CONC under the porch are in the same relative position the distance from the roof to the porch is almost perfect. The rear add-on room is even made in the same manner. How likely is that? The real crowning is the treatment above the lo
3 CONC wer window on the left side - it has a little extension over the window - I don't recall what that kind of treatment is called, but the what chance is there that two different houses would be this close to identical? The chimney o
3 CONC n the right house is shorter, but it is located within reason of the other house and the part that is missing is the ornate portion of the crown. You don't think those houses are the same one? Why would the folks spend so much tim
3 CONC e taking pictures of it otherwise? They drove along the road and took pictures then drove up into the farmyard and took more pictures. This house is distinctly different from the one in the 1912 picture which is the Bergen farm. B
3 CONC ut this one is probably 1910 before they purchased the Bergen farm (1912) based on the size of the boys and the fact that Eileen is not there also.  The angle at which the picture was taken is also almost the same - the distance a
3 CONC way for the right one is maybe 10 times as far. There is clearly a driveway where the buggy is standing so the photographer was definitely closer for the left one. But all of the geometrics - roof slopes, overhangs, sizes and numb
3 CONC ers of windows and doors the shape of the posts attached to the porch - the relative size and alignment of the windows all make them look alike. The only detail that doesn't match up almost perfectly is the two windows above the w
3 CONC indow treatment on the left side - something makes the right house look like those are bugged out a little and it could be due to the out of focus shadowing because all three windows on the right end of the house have extra shadow
3 CONC s as does the fill between the roof and the horizontal on the add-on. They are all the same so probably due to the same thing. I am not saying that this is the Bergen house with the dormer and ornate work on the vertical posts, et
3 CONC c. But these two houses could easily be one and the same.
2 NOTE These are probably the same house. or at least possibly, though they don't really look like it to me. It could be the difference in cameras. Becky
2 NOTE One Chimney. the other one has 2.
2 NOTE Just in case the caption is too hard to read, I am suggesting that these two pictures depict the same house; the left one in about 1910 and the right one in 1962 after years of not being maintained. This is to say, that the folks 
3 CONC believed that this was the original homestead site from their childhood when they took the picture in 1962 (8 mm video). The buildings are identical, location of windows and doors are the same, I don't see the cellar entry which c
3 CONC ould be hidden by the horses in the1910 picture. Both of them have a similar size lean-to back entry or storm entry towards the barn. The chimney is in the correct place, though a little shorter than is shown on the 1910 picture. 
3 CONC The 1910 picture also has another outbuilding close by, which has been removed by the time of the1962 picture.
