1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "You Are My Sonshine" was Joe Wing's favorite song.  He sang it when he milked the cows as a youngster.  He sang it when he shaved.  He sang it to his children, grandchildren and great grandson, teaching them not o
2 CONC nly the words and music, but the philosophy behind it.  Joe Wing was a sunshine person.
2 CONT
2 CONT His birth, October 8, 1906, in Somers, Iowa to farmer parents, was followed with Holy Baptism, making the brightness of his life genuine Sunshine because of Christ.  A life -long Lutheran, he was confirmed in the Church, and spent
2 CONC  much of his life serving on boards and committees and in offices of congregations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following high school in Somers, the family moved to Jackson Minnesota, and Joe went to St. Olaf College in North field to play football and learn important lessons about life itself.  He concluded his education at Mankato Busines
2 CONC s College, and went on to become an accountant for Swift and Company, where he worked for thirty-five years.
2 CONT
2 CONT At business school, he met Gladys Irene Severson, and they were married on a farm east of Bergen, Minnesota, June 29, 1930.  The sunshine brightened as the family grew.  In 1948, the family moved to Lynwood, California, and Joe co
2 CONC ntinued with Swift out here until his retirement in 1966.  In 1969, Joe and Gladys moved to El Toro, delighting in the California sunshine they had been singing about for years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In thirty-five years at Swift, he never missed a day of work until a heart attack broke his record.  Competitive in a cheerful way, Joe liked challenging records.  As an office worker, he liked to see if he could keep up with farm
2 CONC  crews in shocking season, as he had done in his youth. He could.  That zest found him walking to work and home again for years in the bliss of Minnesota springs and the sting of its winters.  The boys remember his face frosted wi
2 CONC th ice and snow, coming home from the office, but the sunshine was still in his eyes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Excellent health in earlier days was complicated by declining health in the last years, although he always remained vigorous in faith and activity.  While Gladys had taken up palette and brush long before, Joe waited until retirem
2 CONC ent to make his masterpieces, and there too the sunshine revealed itself.  He excelled at his avocation.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his wife died February 19, 1982, Joe decided to move to Newport Villa in Newport Beach, where an active retirement would enable him to master his physical problems while enjoying new friendships.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a leader of the Church, he was instrumental in the development of St. Paul's Lutheran Church in Lynwood.  As a Christian in the community, he was one of the first Cub masters as the Boy Scouts developed the Cubbing program and 
2 CONC as a tease, he was one who could bring the sunshine out of all others.  As a child, his mother sighed smilingly as she found eggs sucked clean of their contents under the hens when she was gathering eggs.  Joseph had fooled her wi
2 CONC th a practical joke.  He would hollow out freshly baked loaves too, devouring the delicious cooling bread, to leave his surprised mother with a perfectly shaped crust to slice.  He also had a trick with a re-appearing penny that h
2 CONC e played on everyone with gullible results.  The energy of a wholesome outlook brought not only humor, but vast love to countless others.  He was close to nephews and nieces, as well as his own sons and grandchildren, mixing his f
2 CONC aith, his humor and his sunshine in such a way that others felt its radiant warmth.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to his sons, he leaves grandchildren Michael, Susan,  Lori, Wendy, Dana, Jason, Ryan, and Annamarie, and great grandson Casey. His brothers, Oliver and private in Minnesota, and sister's Eileen Thompson of St. Paul, an
2 CONC d Serene Kratavil of Puyallup, Washington, also survive him.
2 CONT
2 CONT May God continue to shine His rays of comfort upon the family as they remember the Sunshine in His servant Joseph Eli Wing, Sr.
