1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Askel knew the businesses of farmng, tanning, blasting rocks, railroad repair and meat peddling.  At 23 years, Askel married Esther Brattebo and they farmed at Brynnesland, Norway.  Because of rugged terrain and ro
2 CONC cky soil, only 3% of Norway's land couldbe cultivated in the early 19th century.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The family worked together to harvest hay.  Because of 126 inches of rainfall a year in the area of Bergen, Norway, drying the hay was difficult.  To supplement income, the family trapped small animals.  Askel was also a tanner, p
2 CONC reparing these animal hides for commercial use.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1875, Askel inherited Klungland farm, located about seven miles northeast of Egersund, Norway.  The Berkeland family endured troublesome economic conditions, and in 1879, Askel went bankrupt.  At that time the Hetland Township 
2 CONC History #2 (published 1971) listed his assets as 1 horse, 3 cows, 10 sheep, about 560 pouonds of barley, about 5,650 pouonds of oats and 7,910 pounds of potatoes.
2 CONT
2 CONT The discouraged family of 9 moved about 3 miles northeast to Berkeland (Byrkeland).  Askel became a hired farmhand and to supplement income, he served as caretaker for the Helleland Church Cemetery.  In 1882, the oldest daugher, I
2 CONC ngeborg, and oldest son, Bernt, immigrated to Illinois to start a new life.  His second daughter, Rebecca Maria, also went to America to marry Andrew Finnestad.  In 1889, Askel and his family left Norway to join his oldest childre
2 CONC n in America.
